Here's the formal **testsmells.org 19-smell** audit of `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, with integration-test calibration applied (class is tagged `[TestCategory("Integration")]`).

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Total    | 4           | 4              |

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs:87`  
**Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay`

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000); // ← hard-coded 3-second wall-clock wait
```

**Integration calibration:** Even for integration tests, `Thread.Sleep` is a smell — it introduces non-determinism and unnecessarily slows the suite. Downgraded smells like Mystery Guest don't apply here because timing waits are unreliable at any scope.

**Fix:**
```csharp
// Poll with timeout instead of sleeping
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
{
    await Task.Delay(50, cts.Token);
}
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures on slow agents; 3 s unconditional wall-clock cost per run.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs:101-108`  
**Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType`

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

One branch will never execute — the test silently passes on whichever path happens to run, hiding coverage gaps in the other.

**Fix:** Split into two deterministic tests:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — one entire behavioral path is never verified.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs:113-119`  
**Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors`

```csharp
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
{
    _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
}
// ← no assertion anywhere
```

The method name implies "no errors" but the framework will report PASS even if inserts silently corrupt data.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var all = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, all.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False-pass — the test provides coverage metrics with zero defect-detection power.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs:35-38`  
**Method:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase`

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

Four assertions with no message parameters. When one fails in CI, the stack trace shows only `Assert.AreEqual failed` with no indication of *which property* was wrong.

**Fix:** Add assertion messages:
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should persist");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should persist");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Id should be assigned by database");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower failure diagnosis, especially in CI logs.

---

## Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The following tests are well-designed:

| Method | Why it's good |
|--------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, focused assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single behavior, negative assertion is appropriate |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Self-documenting count (3 inserts → assert 3), uses `CollectionAssert` |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Proper async/await, verifies end-state |

The fixture design (in-memory SQLite, `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]`) is also appropriate — no General Fixture smell since all fields are used by every test.

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Effort | Action |
|----------|-------|--------|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | 2 min | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` to `BulkInsert` |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | 5 min | Split `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` into two tests |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | 10 min | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with polling + timeout |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | 3 min | Add message strings to `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` |

**Bottom line:** 4 out of 8 tests are clean. The three High-severity smells (#1–#3) directly risk false-passes or flakiness and should be fixed first. The Assertion Roulette is a quality-of-life improvement for CI diagnosis.
